abstract: >-
  Simulation code and reference results for the paper “Recovery bursts decide: PBFT, HotStuff and Raft under faults on IEEE 802.1Qbv industrial networks” (Nugroho and Sari).
  The repository holds an ns-3 simulation of a 1 ms industrial control loop sharing a time-aware shaped (IEEE 802.1Qbv) network with consensus traffic. It includes PBFT with view changes and Byzantine behaviours, chained HotStuff and Raft. Across 329 configurations, schedules that pass a per-round frame-counting rule still fail after a single fault: a recovery burst can set off a storm of view changes that lasts after the fault ends. Recovery needs about 25% spare capacity after a crash and 40% after equivocation, unless re-proposal or catch-up is bounded. A one-parameter recovery-burst rule accounts for 194 of 214 development configurations and predicted 16 of 17 PBFT outcomes registered before the runs. The control loop never missed a deadline, and no run violated safety.
  Built on ns-3.49 with the DenKrysos TAS module at commit 3d00954. tools/verify_paper2.sh rebuilds the stack, reruns every experiment and checks the results against tools/reference/paper2/. Version 1.x holds the code of the first paper, whose runs still reproduce in this version.
